<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Cuthbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 634–<a href="/years/687">687</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Cuthbert of Lindisfarne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Cuthbert was a Northumbrian monk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, the most venerated saint of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids his community carried his relics to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester-le-street">Chester-le-Street</a> and in 995 to Durham, where his shrine became the centre of the cathedral. The lands and privileges of the community of Saint Cuthbert passed to the bishops of Durham and underpinned their later palatine power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert.jpg" alt="Saint Cuthbert" width="320" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bede · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuthbert_discovers_piece_of_timber_-_Life_of_St._Cuthbert_(late_12th_C),_f.45v_-_BL_Yates_Thompson_MS_26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cuthbert was born in the mid-630s, perhaps into a noble family, in or near Dunbar, then in Anglo-Saxon Northumbria and now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-lothian">East Lothian</a>. The kingdom had been Christian only since the conversion of King Edwin in 627, and Irish monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> had founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> at about the time of Cuthbert's birth. According to his early biographies, he was a shepherd boy who saw the soul of Aidan carried to heaven by angels on the night Aidan died, in 651, and then decided to become a monk. He seems to have served as a soldier first. He joined the new monastery at Melrose under the prior Boisil and later succeeded him as prior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cuthbert served for a time as guest master at the new monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripon">Ripon</a>, but returned to Melrose with Eata when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfrid">Wilfrid</a> was given Ripon. Educated in the Celtic tradition, he accepted the Roman customs after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-whitby">Synod of Whitby</a> in 664 and introduced them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> as prior. He travelled as a preacher as far as Galloway, and gained the name Wonder Worker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for gifts of healing and insight. In 676 he withdrew to a hermit's life, and later to Inner Farne, where he lived austerely and eventually received visitors only through a window.</p><p>In 684 he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a>, but he accepted only after a visit from a large group including King Ecgfrith, and exchanged sees with Eata to become bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>. Archbishop Theodore consecrated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> on 26 March 685. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 686 he returned to his cell on Inner Farne and died there on 20 March 687. He was buried at Lindisfarne the same day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When his coffin was opened eleven years after his death, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> reports, the body was found incorrupt, and his cult grew until he became the most popular saint of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 875 the monks fled from Danish attack, carrying his body to places including Melrose before it came to rest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester-le-street">Chester-le-Street</a>, and in 995 another Danish invasion led to its removal. The community settled at Durham, where a new church was built and the relics were enshrined in 999.</p><p>A small copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-john">Gospel of John</a>, the Saint Cuthbert Gospel, was found in his tomb in 1104, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> gave vestments to the shrine. By the later 11th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Durham held the semi-autonomous Liberty of Durham, and the saint became a symbol of its autonomy; his banner was carried in battle against the Scots until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Henry VIII's commissioners stripped the shrine in the Dissolution of the Monasteries, but his relics remain at the cathedral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cuthbert” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal power depended heavily on the church: kings founded and endowed monasteries, and bishops such as Wilfrid of York, <b>Cuthbert of Lindisfarne</b> and Ecgberht of York were political figures in their own right.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops of Durham inherited the lands and prestige of the community of <b>Saint Cuthbert</b>, whose shrine moved to Durham in 995.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-durham">County Palatine of Durham</a></li><li><blockquote>Bamburgh's lords protected the community of <b>Saint Cuthbert</b>, which moved with its relics to Chester-le-Street and then Durham.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-bamburgh">Earldom of Bamburgh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Smailholm, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Farne Islands, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.62,-2.57&amp;year=668" aria-label="Smailholm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Saint Cuthbert"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4876 5372h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4876 5372h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.62,-2.57&amp;year=668">What was here in 668 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…remember at this time to ask me and speak to me concerning all whereof you have need to ask and speak; for, when we part, we shall never again see one another with bodily eyesight in this world.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to the hermit Herebert at Carlisle (Bede, Ecclesiastical History, book 4, chapter 29) &#183; tr. A. M. Sellar, 1907 &#183; Bede's account of their last meeting &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/38326/pg38326-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let concord be mutual between you and other servants of Christ; and do not despise others who belong to the faith and come to you for hospitality, but admit them familiarly and kindly…&#8221;</p><footer>His last words to the monks of Lindisfarne, 687 (Bede, Life of St. Cuthbert, chapter 39) &#183; tr. J. A. Giles &#183; as the abbot Herefrith, who was with him, told them to Bede &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/bede-cuthbert.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
